  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM EDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nCurrently, ridging surface and aloft are producing another dry and \nmild day across the region.  Plenty of high level cloudiness. \nOverall, nice conditions for outside activities this evening. \n\nFor late Tonight through Sunday night, a short-wave will move across \nthe Ohio and Tennessee valleys. Pressure falls associated with this \nwave will increase the low-level jet over middle Tennessee into the \nPlateau inducing isentropic lift. Strongest lift will be across the \nPlateu counties and southeast Tennessee. Since moisture return will \nbe greatest over these areas, best coverage of showers will be west \nof interstate 75. For areas east of interstate 75, weaker isentropic \nlift and continued dry boundary layer will only allow for areas of \nsprinkles or dry conditions.\n\nA weak mountain wave event will occur across the mountains and \nfoothills for tonight and Sunday but sustained wind speed and gusts \nwill remain below advisory criteria.\n\nFor Monday and Tuesday, upper and surface ridging will produce dry \nand unseasonably warm conditions. Highs will be 5 to 10 degrees \nabove normal.\n\nFor Tuesday night through Friday, a stronger upper trough will move \ninto the Tennessee valley and southern Appalachians. Ensemble \ncluster analysis do show a good deal of variance regarding the \nstrength and timing of this system. Main impacts will be much needed \nrainfall for much of the area along with the potential of mountain \nwave downslope winds into the mountains and foothills. Temperatures \nwill remain near to slightly above normal.\n\nThis system exits the area by next weekend with drier conditions.",
